# infix: a calculator with precedence and parentheses. One expression per line in, its value on a line out.
#
#   4 + 3 * 2        writes 10       * and / bind tighter than + and -
#   (4 + 3) * 2      writes 14       parentheses, as deep as you like
#   2 - 3 - 4        writes -5       the same precedence goes left to right
#   -3 + 5           writes 2        a minus first on a line or straight after ( is 0 minus the term that follows
#   7 / 2            writes 3        division rounds down; dividing by 0 leaves the left side as it was
#
# Non-negative whole numbers, + - * / and ( ), with spaces anywhere between them. The last line needs no
# newline, an empty line writes nothing, and both stacks are empty when the input ends. Numbers are 64-bit
# and wrap.
#
# The idea. No stack of operators and no stack of values: for every group that is open, the line itself
# and each ( inside it, the main stack holds a frame of four numbers on top of a slot S for the group's sum:
#
#   S | sgn T M N      S    the sum of the terms finished so far
#                      sgn  1 or -1: the term being built is added or subtracted
#                      T    the product so far within the term
#                      M    what the next number does to T: 0 multiplies, positive divides
#                      N    the number being read
#
# A new frame is 1 1 0 0: plus one times the number to come. A digit d makes N = 10N + d. Every operator,
# ) and the end of a line first does T = T*N or T/N. Then * and / set M N; + and - add sgn*T to S and start
# a new term; ( pushes a frame; ) adds sgn*T to S, and S lies where the outer N was, which is where the
# group's value belongs; the end of a line does the same and writes S. The stash keeps a 10 at its bottom
# for the whole line: the digit trail copies it for 10N, the operator trail for c - 42, and the writing ring
# writes it last, as the newline.
#
#   ring: empty the stack --> L: read c ----- the end of input: halt
#     ^      ^                 |    '-------- a space, a newline: round again
#     |      '-----------------'
#     |                        v
#     |             the first character: a 10 into the stash, a frame under c
#     |                        v
#     |    M: read c --> c divmod 16 ---- a digit: N = 10N + r ------------------------> M
#     |                    |     |
#     |      the line ends |     '-- x = c - 42 ---- a space --------------------------> M
#     |                    |              |   '----- ( : a frame out of the -2 --------> M
#     |                    v              v
#     |              T = T*N or T/N <-- x divmod 5:  ) -1 4   * 0 0   + 0 1   - 0 3   / 1 0
#     |                    |--- * / : the two numbers are the new M N -----------------> M
#     |                    v
#     |              S = S + sgn*T
#     |                    |--- ) : S is the outer N ----------------------------------> M
#     |                    |--- + - : a new term ---------------------------------------> M
#     |                    v
#     '--- write the stash <-- the digits into the stash <-- the sign
#
# A character is sorted by three pinks, three ways each: on 2 - c/16 (digit, line end, the rest), on
# c - 40 (space, the opening parenthesis, an operator) and, after the arithmetic, on the quotient of
# (c - 42)/5. The end of a line travels the operator trail as an operator of its own, so the arithmetic is
# there once. Six trails come back to the reading black. They meet in a funnel, orange brown orange brown,
# which does nothing: a trail that has one number too many joins at a brown, the others at an orange, and
# no strip has more than two ways in. Three counted rings do the rest: one empties the main stack when a
# line starts, one cuts the digits off into the stash (it goes round to the right: the pink tests q - 2q),
# one writes the stash. The trails never cross.
#
# Malformed input never hangs and leaves nothing behind for the next line. A missing number counts as 0
# (2+*3 writes 2, () writes 0); spaces inside a number are not there; a ( that is never closed makes its
# group the answer; a ) too many gives nonsense; 5(2) writes 7 and (2)3 writes 23; a tab, a carriage return
# or any other control character ends the line, so CRLF lines work; ! " # $ % & ' are skipped like spaces;
# a comma drops the next term and a full stop subtracts it twice; : ; < = > ? and letters are digits worth
# their code modulo 16. -9223372036854775808 is written as -2.
#
# Not supported: a minus straight after an operator (2 * -3 is 2*0 - 3), a plus sign in front of a number,
# powers, decimals, and messages about mistakes.
#
# Made by gen_infix.py from a layout search; do not edit by hand. Every strip that must not link is more
# than 1600 from every other, and every turn is decided by more than 27 degrees.
#
# The start of a line: empty the main stack, then read until something that is not a space.
